            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Case Opening: The Afternoon the Account Got Suspended</h2>
<p>A tool app's overseas team suddenly received a suspension notice from Meta Business Manager on a Wednesday afternoon. The ad account showed "restricted activity violation", all active ads were paused, and BM access was locked. With only 48 hours until the big promotion launch and daily spend over $3,000, the team was thrown into chaos.</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated incident. Account suspensions, ad disapprovals, and payment rejections are challenges every team faces in overseas advertising. But the difference is: some teams recover within 24 hours, while others wait two weeks, losing significant budget and timing. The gap comes down to having a mature compliance audit SOP.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Problem Diagnosis: Quickly Locating Compliance Risks</h2>
<h3>Judgment 1: Suspension Type</h3>
<p>The first step after suspension isn't writing an appeal—it's confirming the suspension type:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Full account suspension</strong>: All advertising features restricted, usually involving serious policy violations or accumulated historical violations</li>
<li><strong>Ad disapprovals</strong>: Individual or multiple ads rejected, account still functional</li>
<li><strong>Payment rejection</strong>: Top-up failures or payment account flagged</li>
</ul>
<p>The team discovered this was a full account suspension, with the notification mentioning "misleading advertising behavior" and "landing page violations".</p>
<h3>Judgment 2: Quick Tracing</h3>
<p>The team immediately traced operations from the past 7 days, identifying three high-risk points:</p>
<ol>
<li>New creative launched last week using unverifiable absolute claims like "#1 rated app"</li>
<li>Landing page was temporarily changed during promotion without updating privacy policy links</li>
<li>Customer service promised unsupported features when replying to user comments</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Execution: Compliance Audit Checklist Within 72 Hours</h2>
<h3>Phase 1: 0-6 Hours — Bleeding Control and Evidence Preservation</h3>
<p><strong>Action taken:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Paused all high-risk creatives (even with account suspended, creative info may be used for secondary review)</li>
<li>Screenshotted all ad data, spend records, account status</li>
<li>Exported historical ad reports as part of appeal materials</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Common mistake:</strong> Many teams repeatedly refresh account status or try recreating ads at this point, which only worsens violation flags.</p>
<h3>Phase 2: 6-24 Hours — Problem List Self-Check</h3>
<p>Use this checklist to inspect item by item:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Audit Item</th>
<th>Check Content</th>
<th>High-Risk Indicators</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Ad creative copy</td>
<td>Absolute claims, false data, inflammatory language</td>
<td>"Guaranteed results"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Landing page compliance</td>
<td>Link validity, privacy policy, terms of service completeness</td>
<td>Dead links, redirect anomalies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Targeting settings</td>
<td>Discriminatory terms, protected groups</td>
<td>Accidental sensitive targeting</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Payment methods</td>
<td>Credit card/PayPal info matching account holder</td>
<td>Mismatched entities</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account history</td>
<td>Unresolved violation records</td>
<td>Accumulated warnings</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Phase 3: 24-72 Hours — Appeal Submission and Follow-up</h3>
<p><strong>Core elements of appeal letter:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Acknowledge the issue</strong>: Clearly state the violation, don't make excuses</li>
<li><strong>Rectification explanation</strong>: List corrective measures already taken</li>
<li><strong>Prevention commitment</strong>: Explain future compliance management mechanisms</li>
<li><strong>Supporting attachments</strong>: Screenshots, data reports, before/after comparison</li>
</ol>
<p>The team submitted their appeal within 24 hours, attaching updated creatives, landing page revision screenshots, and documentation of their newly established creative review process. They received reinstatement notification 48 hours later.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Results and Reflection: The Real Value of SOPs</h2>
<h3>Outcome</h3>
<ul>
<li>Account reinstated within 72 hours, ads resumed normally</li>
<li>Campaign delayed only 1 day, spend loss controlled within 15%</li>
<li>Team established a compliance pre-approval process for creative launches</li>
</ul>
<h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
<p>The value of a compliance audit SOP isn't in "how to rescue when problems occur" but in establishing a prevention mechanism:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pre-launch creative review</strong>: 3-level approval—specialist self-check → supervisor review → compliance checklist confirmation</li>
<li><strong>Landing page changes</strong>: Any page adjustment requires synchronized privacy policy and link validity checks</li>
<li><strong>Regular audits</strong>: Weekly scheduled health checks, clearing historical violation records</li>
<li><strong>Permission management</strong>: Minimize high-risk operation permissions to avoid accidental triggers</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Account suspension is a frequent risk in overseas advertising, but it's preventable. Building a clear compliance audit SOP comes down to three things: <strong>quick problem identification, systematic investigation, and standardized appeals</strong>. When you have an executable process, suspension won't cause panic—it will be handled methodically, minimizing damage as much as possible.</p>
